WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.802
-We're back
with Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.738
And congratulations
on "Tick, Tick...Boom!"

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:07.307
It's receiving
fantastic reviews.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.975
-Thank you.
-So this is based on

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:11.144
the writer of "Rent,"

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.446
but this is not about
this part of his career.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.647
Or that part of his career,
I should say.

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.982
This was
about something earlier.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.818
This was about a failure.
-Yeah, he --

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.822
Jonathan Larson was --
was an aspiring composer.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.857
I can't relate at all.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:27.660
And he spent his 20s
trying to --

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.130
First of all, he tried to do
an adaptation of "1984."

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.264
Couldn't get the rights.

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:34.199
He said,
"I'll write my own dystopia."

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:37.770
He spent his 20s writing
this musical called "Superbia,"

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.973
and everyone basically said,
"It's really interesting.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:43.076
You're very talented.
What else do you have?"

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.945
And he said,
"I spent my 20s writing this."

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.581
And I think he processed
that loss

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:50.283
and the fact that no one
wanted to produce it

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.785
by writing this one-man show,
called "Tick, Tick...Boom!"

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:54.787
And he performed it
as a rock monologue

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:58.223
with a band all over the Village
and all over New York.

00:00:58.223 --> 00:01:01.060
And he, basically, was like --
it was him processing

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.563
spending his 20s on something
nobody wanted to see.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.865
And, as a result,
it's a brilliant

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.167
sort of portrait
of this great artist

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.636
as a young man and figuring out

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.372
whether he wants to proceed
in this line of work or not.

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:15.842
And "Rent" is still
way down on the horizon.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.877
-And you were how old
when you first see this?

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:21.280
-So, I was 17 when I saw "Rent."

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.616
I saw the original cast
for my 17th birthday

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.985
from the back row of
the Nederlander Theatre.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.054
And that was the show that made
me want to write musicals.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:31.290
It was the first, really,
contemporary musical I ever saw.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.626
And then,
after Larson's passing,

00:01:34.626 --> 00:01:36.729
it was commissioned to --

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.264
to be turned into
an off-Broadway show.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:41.000
They basically turned it into a
three-person off Broadway show,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.769
and I saw that.
Now I'm a senior theater major.

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:47.706
I'm like, "I'm a theater major.
It's just after September 11th."

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.577
Like, "What do I
have to offer the world?"

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:53.646
And I went and saw
this brilliant musical,

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:57.149
directed by Scott Schwartz,
starring Raul Esparza,

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:01.186
and it was basically
this sneak preview of my 20s.

00:02:01.186 --> 00:02:04.690
It was this brilliant kind of
wrestling with,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.759
"What are we meant
to be doing with our time?"

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:10.496
And it was even more specific
and brilliant to me

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:12.532
than my experiencing "Rent"
at 17.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:16.101
And so, it's been sitting in me,
like, ever since.

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:19.305
And I was thrilled to get to
direct the film adaptation.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.340
-You get it to direct it, and
you get to take this core story,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.910
but, obviously, because
it's film, in some degrees,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.845
you get to make it bigger,
and one way that you seem

00:02:26.845 --> 00:02:30.583
to make it grander and sort of
a tribute to Jonathan

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.751
is all these Broadway cameos.
-Yeah.

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:34.120
-And -- And it sort of goes back
to what you were saying

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.555
about the speed of thought.
Right?

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:39.292
Like, you get to do -- you get
to internalize this person,

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.960
and it must have been so cool

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.695
to reach out to the Broadway
community and explain

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.363
why it's important
to do a cameo in this film.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.698
-Sure.
And half of the folks

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.066
that people
are considering cameos,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.735
they're not cameos to me.
They're just, like,

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:51.270
great New York actors
that I want to be --

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.504
You know,
you know this from this.

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:53.940
You've got Richard Kind
in every third thing.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.008
-Absolutely.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.209
-Richard Kind
is in our movie, too.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.910
-You can't say no
to Richard Kind.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:58.944
-You can't
say no to Richard Kind.

00:02:58.944 --> 00:03:00.713
-And he will call you.
-Yes.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:01.747
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.349
But, you know, it's -- it's --

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.451
I just wanted
great New York actors

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.853
to be in this great
New York story.

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:11.757
But there are elements to it
that, you know, Jonathan was --

00:03:11.757 --> 00:03:13.092
never got to see his success.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.894
You know, he passed away
the night before

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.896
"Rent's"
first public performance.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:21.433
And the goal at every stage was,
"What would Jonathan Larson do?"

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:23.168
and "What would
Jonathan Larson want to see?"

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:26.405
And the film is an opportunity
to go to New York in 1990,

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:30.710
that sort of post-Koch,
pre-Giuliani weirdo time

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.846
in New York
that I remember as a child

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.149
and populated
with Jonathan's heroes.

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:39.051
I mean, that was sort of the
goal at every step of the way.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.387
-One cameo
that I think people

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.623
were very excited to see
is your father.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:44.857
-Oh, sure.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.625
-When you talk about
New York royalty...

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:48.360
-Yeah.
-...Luis Miranda Jr...

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.329
-When you get Luis,
I'm like, "Is that the Luis?"

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:52.565
-"They -- They booked Luis?"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.200
-No, but how is your dad
to work with as --

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.235
when you're the director?
-Ha!

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.504
[ Laughter ]
-He's -- It -- Well...

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.340
I could've made it
easier on myself,

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.277
because the scene he's in
is this uninterrupted --

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:07.513
It's, like,
my only Scorsese take.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:11.484
It's this, like, long dance
sequence in a New York lobby,

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.753
and I also wanted it
to play like

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:14.987
they're moving in slow motion.

00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:19.792
So all the dancers are dancing
at 1.75 speed.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:23.595
So my dad has a really specific
cue, and it goes by really fast.

00:04:23.595 --> 00:04:25.197
It's like,
♪ Hello to dear Mr. Doorman ♪

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:26.332
♪ Who looks like
Captain Kangaroo ♪

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.334
"And then wave!
And, Daddy, wave!"

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.236
[ Laughter ]
And he's got, like,

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:32.004
this really tough
kind of musical --

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.840
And he'd be like, "I got it!
I got it. Leave me alone."

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.874
You can't direct your parents.
-No, no, no.

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:38.177
-You know, I know
Martin Scorsese put his mom in,

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.179
like, a lot of his movies.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:43.115
-And I don't know how he did it,
because I was just hearing --

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:46.852
Like, around -- after take five,
my dad was like,

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:49.355
"How many more?"
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:52.357
"'Cause I have a real job."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.357 --> 00:04:54.494
-Also, Stephen Sondheim
was a very important person

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:55.861
in Jonathan's life.
-Yes.

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:59.765
-And Bradley Whitford plays him.
I imagine that's something

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:01.233
you don't take on lightly,
to play Stephen Sondheim.

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:03.702
-No. You -- You made it
very difficult for me,

00:05:03.702 --> 00:05:05.070
because in the great
"Co-Op: The Musical" --

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.772
I don't know if you've seen
"Co-Op: The Musical."

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:08.874
"Documentary Now" --
it's a popular show.

00:05:08.874 --> 00:05:12.678
Dozens of fans in the audience!
-Dozens of fans.

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:13.812
-John Mulaney plays Sondheim.

00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:15.648
-Yeah, Mulaney did
a very good Sondheim.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.316
-He did a brilliant Sondheim.
-Yeah.

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:19.184
-So, you know, we --
Luckily, we were in a different

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:23.121
age bracket of Sondheim,
so we cast Bradley as Sondheim.

00:05:23.121 --> 00:05:25.525
-And Sondheim liked it, right?
He liked the performance?

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:28.127
-Yes, he -- he wrote me,
"You treated me gently

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.696
and royally,
for which I am grateful."

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.731
But he had a note,
which is there's --

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:35.968
there's a moment in which
the character of Sondheim

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:39.405
leaves a message for --
for Jonathan Larson,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.473
and I forget
what the sentence was,

00:05:41.473 --> 00:05:43.109
but he was like,
"I would never say that."

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.177
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.712
"Can I rewrite
my voicemail message?"

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.381
-So he thought the way
you presented him in the film,

00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:50.749
even though it was him
being kind,

00:05:50.749 --> 00:05:52.285
was maybe a little cliché?

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:54.286
-Yeah, this one moment,
this one sentence.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:55.387
-Yeah.
-He was like, "I think you have

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.389
a bright future
in this business."

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:58.624
And he was like,
"I would never say

00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:01.094
'bright future
in this business.'"

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:02.595
And so he said,
"Can you rewrite it?

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:06.632
And if you can't get the actor
back to record the voicemail,

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.367
I'll -- I'll --
I'll record myself."

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.002
And I was like,
"I can't get him."

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.503
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.372
"I'm sorry.
Would you please?"

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.708
And so, he -- so we have, like,
a rewrite from Sondheim

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.144
in our movie,
and it's his voice

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.845
on the voicemail
to Jonathan Larson.

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:21.481
-Just this cosmic
reaching across the years.

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:23.215
-Now that's a pretty good cameo.

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:25.651
You know,
with all respect to Luis.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:26.953
[ Applause ]
-Yeah, it's --

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:28.721
that's how you do allegro.
-That's how you do it.

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:30.189
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.624
-Thank you for having me back.
-Congrats on both films.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:33.626
You guys,
that's Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:36.062
"Tick, Tick...Boom!"
available on Netflix.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.398
"Encanto" in theaters
everywhere tomorrow.

